elif [ "$REPO_MODE" -eq 1 ] && [ "$TOPICS_MODE" -eq 1 ]; then
  cat <<INGEST_EOF | (cd "$WT" && "$AGENT" --tools Read,Write,Edit,Bash --context "$WT") || llm_failed
You are ingesting a GitHub repository into an Obsidian wiki. You have the full repo cloned locally for deep analysis.

Wiki location: $WT
Cloned repo: $CLONE_DIR
Repo URL: $INGEST_URL

The repo's README has ALREADY been saved verbatim to raw/$SLUG.md — do NOT refetch it. Read the cloned repo at $CLONE_DIR for code. Do NOT use WebFetch, WebSearch, or any browser tool.

The wiki CLAUDE.md (already loaded for you) defines page format and the layout: raw/ = verbatim, summary/ = precis tagged with topics, note/ = one analysis page per source, topic/ = compiled topic pages that you never write. Follow it.

IMPORTANT: You are one step inside a larger pipeline. Work ONLY inside $WT (plus reading $CLONE_DIR). Do NOT run the wiki-ingest scripts, do NOT use surf or any browser automation, and do NOT run git branch/commit/merge/push — the caller owns all orchestration, commits, and retries.
$EXTRA_NOTES
STEP 0 — VERDICT (do this first): Read raw/$SLUG.md. Decide whether its body is
real repository content (a README/docs), or an error page, access wall, or empty
placeholder.
- If it is NOT real content (BLOCKED): run 'rm raw/$SLUG.md', write NOTHING else,
  and stop. Do not create summary/ or note/ files. Do not edit index.md or log.md.
- If it IS real content (CONTENT): leave raw/$SLUG.md exactly as-is (do not edit
  it) and continue with the analysis below.

$TOPICS_RULE

## Phase 1: Repo exploration

Analyze the cloned repo at $CLONE_DIR. Do NOT just read the README — dig into the actual code:

1. Run: find $CLONE_DIR -type f | head -200 (get the file tree)
2. Run: wc -l on key source files to gauge project size
3. Read the entry point(s) — main.go, index.ts, __main__.py, cmd/, src/main.rs, etc.
4. Read core modules — the files where the actual logic lives, not just config/setup
5. Read any config schemas, API definitions, or protocol files
6. Check dependencies (package.json, go.mod, Cargo.toml, requirements.txt, pyproject.toml)
7. Skim tests if they exist — they reveal intended behavior and edge cases

Spend real time here. Read at least 5-10 source files. The goal is to understand HOW the project works, not just WHAT it claims to do.

## Phase 2: Architectural analysis

Based on your code reading, identify:

- **Architecture pattern**: How is the system structured? (monolith, microservices, pipeline, agent hierarchy, plugin system, etc.)
- **Key techniques**: What are the non-obvious implementation choices? (specific algorithms, data structures, protocols, concurrency patterns)
- **Innovation points**: What does this project do differently from the obvious approach? What would surprise a senior engineer?
- **Design trade-offs**: What did the authors optimize for, and what did they sacrifice? (speed vs correctness, simplicity vs flexibility, etc.)
- **Core abstractions**: What are the 2-3 key types/interfaces/concepts that everything else is built on?

For AI/agent repos specifically, also identify:
- Memory architecture (flat, hierarchical, episodic, semantic, etc.)
- Agent topology (single agent, multi-agent, hierarchy, swarm, etc.)
- Context management strategy (how does it handle long context, summarization, retrieval)
- Tool/capability model (fixed tools, dynamic tool selection, tool composition)
- Prompt engineering patterns (chain-of-thought, few-shot, structured output, etc.)

## Phase 3: Wiki page creation

Leave raw/$SLUG.md exactly as-is (the verbatim README). Then produce:

1. summary/$SLUG.md — a concise precis of the project (frontmatter with url/title/author/dates and the topics: block list described above, then a few short paragraphs). Same slug as the raw file.
2. note/<Title>.md — the analysis page, ONE new file under note/, with:
   - One-paragraph summary of what this project is and why it matters
   - **Architecture** section — how it's built, with specific file/module references
   - **Key techniques** section — the non-obvious stuff, with code-level specifics
   - **Design decisions** section — what trade-offs were made and why
   - **Comparison notes** — how this approach differs from related projects in the wiki (check index.md)
   - Tags: #tool #project plus domain tags (#agents #database #memory etc.)
   - [[wikilinks]] to related pages, and a source footer linking [[raw/$SLUG]] and [[summary/$SLUG]]
   Read index.md for cross-link targets and grep existing note/ and topic/ pages for related keywords.
   <Title> must be a filename that does not already exist under note/ or topic/; if it does, pick a more specific title.
3. RELATE — connect this project to the wiki from YOUR OWN note. Find the 2–4
   MOST-related existing pages under note/ or topic/ (use index.md and grep) and
   link to each with a [[wikilink]] from the note you just wrote, in a sentence
   that says how this project compares to or complicates that page.

   Do NOT edit any existing note/ or topic/ page. Not even additively. Cross-page
   curation is a SEPARATE COMPILE STEP (wiki-recompile), which rebuilds a topic
   page from ALL of its sources at once. The link you write above carries the
   same information, and Obsidian derives the backlink for free.
4. Update index.md — add one entry, '- [[<Title>]] — one line', under the section
   whose heading is your PRIMARY topic's title (index.md has one '## <Topic Title>'
   section per topic; the '## Topics' section at the top lists the compiled pages
   and is not for notes). Do not add a section heading.
5. Append to log.md — exactly ONE bullet line, EXACTLY this format:
   - $INGEST_DATE: Ingested [Title]($INGEST_URL) (author, site, publication-date) — 1–3 sentence summary. → raw/$SLUG.md, [[Page Title]]. Cross-links: [[A]], [[B]].

## BEFORE YOU FINISH — output checklist (do not skip this)

The three phases above are long, and the files that matter most are easy to
lose track of by the time you get here. Check each one on disk before you stop:

- [ ] summary/$SLUG.md EXISTS. This is the precis from Phase 3 item 1. It is the
      single most commonly dropped output. An analysis with no summary/$SLUG.md
      is REJECTED by the caller and the entire ingest fails.
- [ ] summary/$SLUG.md's frontmatter has topics: — one or two slugs from the
      table, block form, primary first.
- [ ] Exactly ONE new page under note/: note/<Title>.md. NOT under topic/, NOT
      <Title>.md at the wiki root. A page written under topic/ or to the root
      fails the ingest.
- [ ] raw/$SLUG.md is unchanged (verbatim README, never rewritten); topics.md is
      unchanged.
- [ ] index.md has the new entry under the primary topic's section; log.md has
      exactly one new bullet.

Write NOTHING to the wiki root. The ONLY root files you may touch are index.md
and log.md. Every page you create belongs in summary/ or note/.

Guidelines:
- Be specific: "uses a skip list for the index" not "uses efficient data structures"
- Be comparative: "unlike X which does Y, this project does Z because..."
- Be opinionated: if the architecture has a weakness or a clever insight, say so
- Keep pages phone-readable: short paragraphs, clear headers
- Today's date is $INGEST_DATE
